.TH VELVET.YML 5 2026-08-03 Velvet "Velvet Manual"
.SH NAME
velvet.yml \- configuration format for a Velvet installation
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I velvet.yml
sits at the root of a Velvet installation and is the single configuration the
monitor, the browser setup, the build Action, and the status page all read. The
complete file is validated before Velvet checks an endpoint, changes an Issue,
or publishes generated data, and unknown fields are rejected rather than
ignored.
.PP
A minimal file needs a display name and a URL:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
schemaVersion: 1
repository:
  owner: your-username
  name: your-status-repo
statusPage:
  name: Example Status
services:
  - name: Website
    url: https://example.com
.fi
.RE
.PP
That configuration sends a direct IPv4
.B GET
request and considers a final HTTP
.B 200
healthy.
.PP
.B repository.owner
and
.B repository.name
must match the repository the workflow runs in. A mismatch stops the run before
any check or repository change.
.PP
Stable service and check identifiers are derived from their names as lowercase
kebab-case. Set an explicit
.B id
before renaming a service or a check whose historical identity has to stay
unchanged.
.SH TOP-LEVEL FIELDS
.TP
.B schemaVersion
Required. The configuration contract version, currently
.BR 1 .
.TP
.B repository
Required. Holds
.B owner
and
.BR name ,
being the GitHub user or organisation and the repository the installation lives
in.
.TP
.B statusPage
Required. The public identity, presentation, navigation, and SEO of
the page.
.TP
.B services
Required. At least one public service, each with one or more HTTP checks.
.TP
.B incidents
Optional. Confirmation thresholds and the GitHub labels Velvet marks its own
Issues with.
.TP
.B history
Optional. The retention policy for generated history.
.TP
.B updates
Optional. The preference for compatible managed security updates.
.TP
.B gallery
Optional. Whether this installation is named on the Velvet website.
.SH SERVICES AND CHECKS
A service takes exactly one of two forms. The compact form names a single
.B url
and creates one check whose identifier and name come from the service. The
explicit form names
.B checks
instead and cannot be combined with
.BR url .
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
services:
  - id: api
    name: Public API
    checks:
      - id: readiness
        name: Readiness
        url: https://api.example.com/ready
      - id: version
        name: Version
        url: https://api.example.com/version
        method: HEAD
        expectedStatusCodes: [200, 204]
        maxRedirects: 2
        timeoutMs: 5000
.fi
.RE
.SS Service fields
.TP
.B id
Optional, derived from the name. A stable lowercase kebab-case identifier of at
most 64 characters.
.TP
.B name
Required. The public service name, at most 128 characters.
.TP
.B url
The compact single-check form. Cannot be combined with
.BR checks .
.TP
.B checks
The explicit form, holding one or more named checks. Cannot be combined with
.BR url .
.SS Check fields
.TP
.B id
Optional, derived from the name. A stable lowercase kebab-case identifier,
unique within its service.
.TP
.B name
Required. The public check name.
.TP
.B url
Required. An absolute HTTP or HTTPS URL. Credentials and fragments are
rejected.
.TP
.B method
Optional, defaulting to
.BR GET .
Either
.B GET
or
.BR HEAD .
.TP
.B expectedStatusCodes
Optional, defaulting to
.BR [200] .
One to 32 unique final status codes from 100 through 599.
.TP
.B maxRedirects
Optional, defaulting to
.BR 5 .
A redirect limit from 0 through 10.
.TP
.B timeoutMs
Optional, defaulting to
.BR 10000 .
The absolute timeout across all redirects, from 100 through 60000 milliseconds.
.TP
.B headers
Optional, empty by default. Up to 16 header names carrying secret references.
.TP
.B jsonAssertions
Optional, empty by default. Up to 16 explicit assertions about a JSON response.
.SS What counts as downtime
Each check gets one initial attempt and at most one immediate retry. A status
outside
.BR expectedStatusCodes ,
a DNS or TLS failure, a timeout, a failed assertion, or an invalid response is
an unavailable measurement.
.PP
Invalid configuration, a missing configured secret, an unsafe request setup, a
cancelled run, and internal errors are not. Velvet publishes nothing in those
cases rather than claim the endpoint was down.
.SS JSON assertions
By default Velvet looks at the status code and does not read the body at all.
Use
.B jsonAssertions
where an endpoint deliberately reports its own health as JSON.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
jsonAssertions:
  - path: /status
    equals: ok
  - path: /dependencies/database/ready
    equals: true
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B path
is an RFC 6901 JSON Pointer, and
.B equals
accepts a string, a number, a boolean, or null. Every assertion has to match.
Velvet reads at most 64 KiB of an asserted response and never infers a schema
from arbitrary content.
.PP
.B HEAD
cannot be combined with assertions, because a
.B HEAD
response carries no body.
.SS Header secrets
A secret value never belongs in
.IR velvet.yml .
Reference the name of the environment variable instead.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
headers:
  - name: Authorization
    secret: API_HEALTH_TOKEN
.fi
.RE
.PP
Map that repository secret explicitly into both monitor workflow steps rather
than passing every repository secret to the Action. Secret interpolation such
as
.B $TOKEN
inside the configuration is rejected. Request-routing, framing, and connection
headers such as
.BR Host ,
.BR Content-Length ,
and
.B Transfer-Encoding
cannot be configured, and configured headers are removed on a cross-origin
redirect.
.SH STATUS PAGE
.TP
.B name
Required. The public page name and the default document title.
.TP
.B customDomain
Optional, defaulting to the GitHub Pages URL. A hostname only, with no scheme,
path, port, credentials, or wildcard.
.TP
.B logoUrl
Optional. The logo shown in the header instead of the page name. Either an
absolute HTTP or HTTPS address, or a file in this repository written as
.BR ./logo.svg .
The browser setup uploads one into the repository and writes the path here.
.TP
.B logoHeight
Optional, defaulting to
.BR 72 .
A display height from 16 through 256 pixels.
.TP
.B showPoweredBy
Not a setting. Every page carries the Powered by Velvet credit and the line
below it naming where the page is configured, and a configuration stating this
field is refused.
.TP
.B layout
Optional, defaulting to
.BR grouped .
Either
.B grouped
for one shared service card or
.B cards
for one card per service.
.TP
.B defaultRange
Optional, defaulting to
.BR 30d .
The initial range, being
.BR 30d ,
.BR 90d ,
or
.BR all .
The last of these reaches back to the day the installation began measuring, so
its length follows the installation rather than being stated here.
A visitor's own saved choice wins afterwards.
.TP
.B responseChart
Optional, defaulting to
.BR true .
Whether each service shows its response-time chart.
Set to
.BR false ,
the readings are still measured and still published and no theme draws them.
It is a page setting rather than a theme one, so it survives a change of the
theme the page is published in.
.TP
.B navigation
Optional, empty by default. Up to 16 links, each with
.B title
and
.BR href .
.TP
.B theme
Required. The theme the page is published in, named by its directory:
.BR velvet ,
.BR retro-chassis ,
.BR twenty-forty-nine ,
or
.BR ncc-1701-d .
A page is published in a theme, so there is no default and a configuration
naming none is refused. A name no installed theme answers to stops the build
rather than publishing a different theme under your domain.
.TP
.B themeSettings
Optional, defaulting to what the theme itself states. What has been set on that
theme, keyed by the setting it belongs to. Which settings a theme has is the
theme's own answer, so an unknown one stops the build and names what that theme
offers.
.TP
.B icons
Optional, chosen automatically. A map from stable service identifier to a
Phosphor icon class.
.TP
.B seo
Optional, generated by default. Title, description, and social-image overrides.
.PP
Setting
.B customDomain
writes a
.I CNAME
file into every build. The repository setting alone changes no DNS, so the
required record has to be added with the domain provider. Do not remove the
GitHub Pages domain until the custom domain resolves and its certificate is
active.
.SS Service icons
.B icons
maps the stable service identifier to a Phosphor class. Browser setup offers
the supported set visually, and an unlisted service uses
.BR ph-circle .
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
icons:
  website: ph-globe
  api: ph-brackets-curly
  database: ph-database
.fi
.RE
.SS SEO
.RS 4
.nf
seo:
  title: Example System Status
  description: Current availability for Example.
  image: https://example.com/status-social.png
.fi
.RE
.PP
Velvet offers no analytics. A generated status page loads no third-party script
and reports to nobody, and there is no setting that would make it.
.PP
Without
.B seo
overrides, each build derives the
.BR title ,
the
.BR description ,
the canonical URL, the Open Graph and Twitter metadata, a 1200 by 630 social
.BR image ,
.IR robots.txt ,
and
.I sitemap.xml
from the page configuration and the latest validated status.
.SH THEMES
A page is published in a theme, and the theme is the whole of what it looks
like. It brings its own colours, its own typefaces and its own shapes, and it
ships them with the page, so a published page asks nobody else for a font.
.PP
Velvet ships four:
.TP
.B velvet
A dark indigo readout, one card per group of services, and a strip of days
under each of them.
.TP
.B retro-chassis
A rack of separate components, each service a brushed faceplate bolted between
two walnut cheeks, with a lit name plate, two protocol lamps, a two-line
readout and a lamp meter for its days.
.TP
.B twenty-forty-nine
A filthy pane of glass with a dim blue readout: corner brackets, edge scales,
dotted grids and a vignette to black.
.TP
.B ncc-1701-d
A divided column of coloured segments carrying the service names, two limbs
enclosing the notices and the readings, and a table of events rather than a
stack of cards.
.PP
A theme says for itself what can be set on it, and different themes offer
different things. What is set goes under
.BR themeSettings ,
keyed by the name of the setting.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
statusPage:
  name: Example Status
  theme: velvet
  themeSettings:
    chartWash: false
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B velvet
offers
.BR chartWash ,
the wash under the response curve. A setting the named theme does not have
stops the build and names what that theme does offer, and so does a value it
cannot take. Nothing is silently ignored.
.PP
There is no palette to write and no typeface to name. Both belong to the theme,
which is what lets one ship whole rather than being assembled from a colour
list somebody wrote by hand.
.SH INCIDENTS AND MAINTENANCE
.RS 4
.nf
incidents:
  failureThreshold: 2
  recoveryThreshold: 2
  incidentLabel: incident
  maintenanceLabel: maintenance
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B failureThreshold
Defaults to
.BR 2 .
Consecutive failed measurements, from 1 through 20.
.TP
.B recoveryThreshold
Defaults to
.BR 2 .
Consecutive successful measurements, from 1 through 20.
.TP
.B incidentLabel
Defaults to
.BR incident .
A lowercase kebab-case GitHub label.
.TP
.B maintenanceLabel
Defaults to
.BR maintenance .
A lowercase kebab-case GitHub label.
.PP
A first failed measurement is pending and shows as degraded. Reaching the
failure threshold confirms the outage and opens one marked Issue. A recovered
target counts as available immediately whilst the displayed state waits for the
recovery threshold, and confirmed recovery adds one comment and closes the same
Issue. Closing that Issue by hand during an active outage reopens it, and
unrelated Issues are never touched.
.PP
Planned maintenance arrives through the generated Issue Form or the maintenance
workflow, and Velvet validates the service identifiers and timestamps in it.
Monitoring continues throughout. An outage covered by a window opens no
incident until the window ends, measured availability is never rewritten, and
the window stays a neutral event in public history.
.SH HISTORY
.RS 4
.nf
history:
  retentionDays: 365
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B retentionDays
Accepts 1 through 365 and defaults to
.BR 365 .
.PP
The same window applies to public daily availability, response samples,
resolved incidents, completed maintenance, private transition history, and
generated branch history. Open incidents and scheduled or active maintenance
stay visible past it, and historical GitHub Issues are never deleted.
.PP
Once retained Git history exceeds the configured period, the current complete
snapshot becomes a new root, taken with an exact lease against the branch head
that was read. The default branch is never force-pushed.
.SH BEING NAMED ON THE VELVET WEBSITE
Off unless you say otherwise.
.PP
.RS
.nf
gallery:
  listed: true
.fi
.RE
.PP
With
.B listed
on, the status page is named at velvet.li/references. The browser setup asks
during its first step, so nothing is listed without being asked, and changing
the answer here takes effect within the hour.
.PP
Velvet discloses the page name and its address, and nothing else. Whether the
repository behind it is public or private makes no difference.
.SH UPDATES
.RS 4
.nf
updates:
  automaticSecurityUpdates: true
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B automaticSecurityUpdates
Enabled by default. Applies only to releases classified as security updates
that need neither a configuration nor a data migration.
.PP
A release installs unattended only whilst every one of the following holds, and
any single failure means it waits for confirmation. It is classified as a
security release. It is explicitly marked eligible for automatic installation,
and a release marked eligible whilst not being a migration-free security
release is refused at publication. It needs neither a configuration nor a data
migration. The installation still has
.B automaticSecurityUpdates
enabled. Its recorded template revision is immutable and every file matches the
hash the release recorded for it.
.PP
An automatic update that fails is not retried for that version, so it will not
open the same branch and pull request repeatedly.
.PP
An update may change a fixed list of files and nothing else: the workflows
Velvet installed, the Issue templates it installed, and
.IR velvet.lock.json ,
which it maintains for you.
Everything else is yours and is never a target, including
.IR velvet.yml ,
the whole
.B velvet-data
branch, incidents, maintenance history, repository secrets, Pages and domain
settings,
.IR README.md ,
and
.IR LICENSE .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I velvet.yml
This file, at the root of the default branch.
.TP
.I velvet.lock.json
The machine-managed record of the installed release. Never edited by hand.
.TP
.I .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/maintenance.yml
The generated maintenance Issue Form. Its choices and embedded identifiers have
to match the configured services, which browser setup handles during
installation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR velvet (7)
.PP
The complete configuration reference is at
.UR https://velvet.li
velvet.li
.UE
and in
.I documentation/configuration.md
in the repository.
